<DocsLayout title="Preview">
  <p>WYSIWYG mode renders formatted text directly where you type. Split View places literal Markdown source on the left and an editable WYSIWYG view of the same document on the right.</p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>By default, syntax markers (<code>**</code>, <code>#</code>, <code>`</code>) appear in WYSIWYG when the cursor enters a formatted region. Choose <strong>View → Show Markup Hints</strong> to turn that behavior off or on. MarkRust saves this preference; the Markdown file is unchanged.</p>

  <h2>Selection</h2>
  <p>With markup hints on, selecting text that overlaps formatted regions reveals the underlying syntax. In Split View, the left pane always shows the literal source.</p>

  <h2>Plain text mode</h2>
  <p>Files with a <code>.txt</code> extension bypass Markdown parsing entirely. No formatting, no delimiter masking — raw text only.</p>

  <h2>Export</h2>
  <p>For a full HTML render, use the CLI:</p>
  <pre><code>markrust export document.md -o document.html</code></pre>
  <p>Exports omit raw HTML and dangerous URL schemes by default. The CLI’s <code>--unsafe-html</code> option is available only for trusted Markdown that intentionally needs raw HTML; GFM tag filtering still applies. Or choose <strong>Export HTML</strong> from the command palette (<kbd>⌘P</kbd>) when a saved document is open.</p>
</DocsLayout>
